-- title:
--   Atkin–Lehner quotient at r goes to a Pi-translate
-- statement:
--   The setting is fixed by several groups of hypotheses.
--
--   **Arithmetic base.** Primes $r$ and $rbar$ with $rbar \neq r$, a nonzero level $N$ with $r \nmid N$; a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal{O}$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi$, with $\mathcal{O}$ adically complete for $(\pi)$, residue ring of cardinality $r$, and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals (so $\mathcal{O}$ is absolutely unramified with residue field of $r$ elements); $K_0$ a characteristic-zero fraction field of $\mathcal{O}$. Further, a characteristic-zero domain $Onr$ which is an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra, together with an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra automorphism $Fr$, such that $Onr$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, that ideal is maximal, every element of $Onr$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal{O}$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $Onr$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $Fr(x) \equiv x^r \pmod{\pi}$ for all $x$ (`hFr`).
--
--   **Quaternionic data.** Rationals $a, b$ with `hB`: $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ is indefinite ($0 < a$ or $0 < b$) and, for each height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb{Q}$, its completion at $v$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ contains $r$ or $rbar$. A $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule $\Lambda$ which is a maximal order (`hΛ`) containing all rational integers (`hΛℤ`), and a map $\mathrm{coord} \colon \Lambda \to \mathrm{Zp2}\,r \times \mathrm{Zp2}\,r$ satisfying `IsOrderCoord` (`hcoord`): additive, sending $1$ to $(1,0)$, multiplicative for the twisted rule $(\alpha,\beta)(\alpha',\beta') = (\alpha\alpha' + r\,\beta\,\sigma\beta',\ \alpha\beta' + \beta\,\sigma\alpha')$ with $\sigma$ the Witt-vector Frobenius, injective, with dense image in the $r$-adic sense, and compatible with reduced traces.
--
--   **Base point and its formal module.** A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over $Onr/\pi$ with $\Lambda$-action and level $N$, a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $X_0$ over $Onr/\pi$ of height $4$ (`hX₀`), formal coordinates $\theta_0$ in two variables along the unit section of $A_0$, and `hA₀`: $\theta_0$ are formal coordinates for the group law of $A_0$ with formal group $X_0.F$, and the $\Lambda$-action on infinitesimal points is computed by the series $\mathrm{addVia}(X_0.\mathrm{act}(\mathrm{coord}(m)_1), X_0.\mathrm{act}(\mathrm{coord}(m)_2) \circ X_0.\varpi)$.
--
--   **Drinfeld datum.** A ring homomorphism $\iota \colon \mathrm{Zp2}\,r \to Onr$; a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $\Phi$ over $Onr/(r)$ which is special for the induced structure map (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`); a moduli package $M$ over $Onr$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hM`); a family $\eta$ assigning to each ring $B$ with a homomorphism $\psi \colon Onr \to B$ in which $r$ is nilpotent a map from rigidified objects $\mathrm{Rigidified}\,r\,\Phi\,B$ (a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $X$ over $B$, a natural number $n$, and a series $\rho$ over $B/(r)$) to $M.\mathrm{obj}$; and `hη`, three clauses: two admissible rigidified objects have the same image under $\eta$ precisely when they are isomorphic in the sense of `Rigidified.IsIsomorphic`; $\eta$ commutes with base change along ring homomorphisms compatible with the legs; and every point of $M.\mathrm{obj}$ becomes, after passing to a finite cover of the base by localisations away from a unit-generating family, the image under $\eta$ of an admissible rigidified object. Also a ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the endomorphisms $\Phi.\mathrm{actEnd}$ and $\Phi.\mathrm{varpiEnd}$ into $M_2(K_0)$, with `hE₀`: $E_0$ is injective and for some $m$ the image of $E_0$ contains $r^m M_2(\mathcal{O})$ and $r^m E_0$ lands in $M_2(\mathcal{O})$. Finally a ring homomorphism $\kappa \colon Onr/\pi \to Onr/(r)$ compatible with the two reductions (`hκ`), a natural number $n_0$ and a series $\beta_0$ over $Onr/(r)$ which is an isogeny $\Phi \to X_0 \otimes_\kappa Onr/(r)$ of height $4n_0$ (`hβ₀`).
--
--   **The dictionary $\Xi$.** For every Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $B$, every $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\psi \colon Onr \to B$ and every witness that $\pi$ is nilpotent in $B$, a map $\Xi$ from rigidified curves $\mathrm{RigidifiedCurve}\,r\,\pi\,A_0\,B\,\psi$ (a fake elliptic curve $E$ over $B$ together with a rigidification: a curve $E_b$ over $B/\pi$ pulled back from $E$, a curve $A_b$ over $B/\pi$ pulled back from $A_0$ along $\mathrm{residueLeg}\,\pi\,\psi$, an exponent $d$, and an isogeny pair $\varphi, \varphi'$ of degree $r^d$ between $E_b$ and $A_b$ preserving the level structure) to $\mathcal{O}$-points $\mathrm{ModuliPackage.GPoint}$ of $M$ over $B$, subject to four clauses: `hΞleg`, over a connected $B$ the leg of $\Xi(x)$ is $\mathrm{frobTwist}\,Onr\,Fr\,k\,\psi = \psi \circ Fr^{k}$ for some $k \in \mathbb{Z}$; `hΞnat`, $\Xi$ is compatible with pullback of a rigidified curve along an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\varphi \colon B \to B'$; `hΞiso`, $\Xi$ takes the same value on two rigidified curves over $(B,\psi)$ whose underlying curves are isomorphic compatibly with group law, $\Lambda$-action and level (`IsoVia`) and whose rigidifications are matched by data $i_b, u_A$ and exponents $i_1, j_1$ making the two isogenies agree after composing with multiplication by $r^{i_1}$, resp. $r^{j_1}$; and `hΞdef`, the defining formula: over a connected $B$ in which $r$ is nilpotent, for a rigidified curve $x$ whose underlying curve carries a formal module structure $(X,\theta)$ via $\mathrm{coord}$, there are $j \le 1$ and a rigidified object $t$ with $t.X = X$, with $t$ obtained from $\theta_0$, $\kappa$, $\beta_0$ and the rigidification of $x$ by `IsRigTransport` at index $j$, with $t$ admissible for $\iota$ and the leg $\mathrm{frobTwist}\,Onr\,Fr\,(-j)\,\psi$, and with $\Xi(x)$ equal to the $G$-point with that leg and point $\eta(t)$.
--
--   **Frobenius twist of the base point.** A fake elliptic curve $A_0^{(r)}$ over $Onr/\pi$ together with $\mathrm{prA} \colon A_0^{(r)}.A \to A_0.A$ exhibiting $A_0^{(r)}$ as the pullback of $A_0$ along $\mathrm{residueLeg}\,\pi\,Fr$ (`hprA`), maps $F \colon A_0.A \to A_0^{(r)}.A$ and $V \colon A_0^{(r)}.A \to A_0.A$ over $Onr/\pi$, and `hFV`, nine clauses: $F$ and $V$ are homomorphisms for the relative group laws, both commute with the $\Lambda$-actions, both carry level points to level points, $V \circ F$ and $F \circ V$ act as multiplication by $r$ on points of $A_0$, resp. of $A_0^{(r)}$, and for every commutative ring $C$ of characteristic $r$ and every point $x \colon \operatorname{Spec} C \to A_0.A$ one has $x$ followed by $F$ followed by $\mathrm{prA}$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the $r$-power Frobenius of $C$ followed by $x$; that is, $F$ followed by $\mathrm{prA}$ is the relative Frobenius.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $B$, every $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\psi \colon Onr \to B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent in $B$, $B$ connected in the sense that every idempotent of $B$ is $0$ or $1$; for all fake elliptic curves $E, E'$ over $B$ of level $N$ with $\Lambda$-action, and maps $q \colon E.A \to E'.A$ and $q' \colon E'.A \to E.A$ over $B$ such that $(q,q')$ presents $E'$ as the Atkin–Lehner quotient of $E$ at $r$, i.e. `IsAtkinLehnerQuotientVia`: both maps are homomorphisms for the group laws, both commute with the $\Lambda$-actions, $q$ followed by $q'$ equals the action of $r$ on $E$ and $q'$ followed by $q$ the action of $r$ on $E'$ (conditionally on $r \in \Lambda$), a point $P$ of $E$ is killed by $q$ exactly when $P$ is killed by every $m \in \Lambda$ with $m \bar m = rn$ for some $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, and $q$ carries level points to level points; and for every rigidification $\rho$ of $E$ with leg $\psi$ and every rigidification $\rho'$ of $E'$ with leg $\mathrm{frobTwist}\,Onr\,Fr\,1\,\psi = \psi \circ Fr$: if the relative-Frobenius correspondence between $\rho$ and $\rho'$ holds, namely that there exist $q_b \colon \rho.E_b.A \to \rho'.E_b.A$ over $B/\pi$ with $q_b$ followed by $\rho'.g_b$ equal to $\rho.g_b$ followed by $q$, a map $u_A \colon \rho'.A_b.A \to A_0^{(r)}.A$ exhibiting $\rho'.A_b$ as the pullback of $A_0^{(r)}$ along $\mathrm{residueLeg}\,\pi\,\psi$ with $u_A$ followed by $\mathrm{prA}$ equal to $\rho'.g_A$, a map $F_b \colon \rho.A_b.A \to \rho'.A_b.A$ over $B/\pi$ with $F_b$ followed by $u_A$ equal to $\rho.g_A$ followed by $F$, and natural numbers $i, j$ with
--   $$q_b \;\text{then}\; \rho'.\varphi \;\text{then}\; [r^i]_{\rho'.A_b} \;=\; \rho.\varphi \;\text{then}\; F_b \;\text{then}\; [r^j]_{\rho'.A_b},$$
--   then
--   $$\mathrm{ModuliPackage.G.IsPiTranslate}\ \iota\ \Phi\ \eta\ Fr\ \bigl(\Xi_{B,\psi}(E,\rho)\bigr)\ \bigl(\Xi_{B,\psi \circ Fr}(E',\rho')\bigr).$$
--   By definition this asserts two things: first, the leg of the second $G$-point is $\mathrm{frobTwist}\,Onr\,Fr\,1$ of the leg of the first; second, the predicate `LocallyLifts` holds for $\iota$, $\Phi$, $\eta$, the two legs, the nilpotence witness and the two points of $M$, taken with respect to the relation `Rigidified.IsPiTranslate`, which for rigidified objects $t$, $t'$ over $B$ requires $t'.X.F = t.X.F$, $t'.X.\varpi = t.X.\varpi$, $t'.X.\mathrm{act}(\alpha) = t.X.\mathrm{act}(\sigma\alpha)$ for all $\alpha$ with $\sigma$ the Witt-vector Frobenius, and the existence of $c \in \mathbb{N}$ with
--   $$\bigl(t.\bar X.\mathrm{act}(r^{\,c + t.n})\bigr) \circ \bigl(t'.\rho \circ (X_i \mapsto X_i^{\,r})\bigr) \;=\; \bigl(t.\bar X.\mathrm{act}(r^{\,c + t'.n})\bigr) \circ \bigl(t.\rho \circ \Phi.\varpi\bigr)$$
--   over $B/(r)$, the series $\Phi.\varpi$ being reduced along the leg.
--
--   This is the formal-module half of the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison at the prime $r$: under the relative-Frobenius correspondence between rigidifications, the supersingular dictionary $\Xi$ carries an Atkin–Lehner quotient at $r$ to a $\Pi$-translate on the Drinfeld side (Boutot–Carayol, II (9.4)–(9.5)). It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.exists_rigidification_frobTwist_isPiTranslate_of_isAtkinLehnerQuotient`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.exists_rigidification_frobTwist_isPiTranslate_of_isAtkinLehnerQuotient), which produces the rigidification $\rho'$ on the quotient curve together with this translation property.
